#: src/modules/paths.md:6
msgid "use, super, self"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:8
msgid ""
"A module can bring symbols from another module into scope with `use`. You "
"will typically see something like this at the top of each module:"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:12 src/modules/paths.md:38
msgid "// Copyright 2022 Google LLC\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:13 src/modules/paths.md:39
msgid "// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:19
msgid "Paths"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:21
msgid "Paths are resolved as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:23
msgid "As a relative path:"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:24
msgid "`foo` or `self::foo` refers to `foo` in the current module,"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:25
msgid "`super::foo` refers to `foo` in the parent module."
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:27
msgid "As an absolute path:"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:28
msgid "`crate::foo` refers to `foo` in the root of the current crate,"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:29
msgid "`bar::foo` refers to `foo` in the `bar` crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:34
msgid ""
"It is common to \"re-export\" symbols at a shorter path. For example, the "
"top-level `lib.rs` in a crate might have"
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:47
msgid ""
"making `DiskStorage` and `NetworkStorage` available to other crates with a "
"convenient, short path."
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:50
msgid ""
"For the most part, only items that appear in a module need to be `use`'d. "
"However, a trait must be in scope to call any methods on that trait, even if "
"a type implementing that trait is already in scope. For example, to use the "
"`read_to_string` method on a type implementing the `Read` trait, you need to "
"`use std::io::Read`."
msgstr ""

#: src/modules/paths.md:56
msgid ""
"The `use` statement can have a wildcard: `use std::io::*`. This is "
"discouraged because it is not clear which items are imported, and those "
"might change over time."
msgstr ""
